1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:08,184
Can you show me resource requests and limits?

2
00:00:08,733 --> 00:00:14,296
Every container can declare how much CPU and memory it needs:

3
00:00:14,296 --> 00:00:15,117
requests:

4
00:00:15,117 --> 00:00:18,126
what the container is guaranteed.

5
00:00:18,126 --> 00:00:24,966
The scheduler only places a Pod on a node that still has its requests free.

6
00:00:24,966 --> 00:00:25,604
limits:

7
00:00:25,604 --> 00:00:27,428
the most it may use.

8
00:00:27,428 --> 00:00:37,003
Above the CPU limit it is slowed down (throttled); above the memory limit the kernel kills it (OOMKilled,

9
00:00:37,003 --> 00:00:38,371
out of memory).

10
00:00:38,371 --> 00:00:38,919
Units:

11
00:00:38,919 --> 00:00:42,658
CPU in cores or millicores (1 = one core,

12
00:00:42,658 --> 00:00:50,318
100m = 0.1 core); memory in bytes with binary suffixes (128Mi = 128 mebibytes, 1Gi).

13
00:00:50,867 --> 00:00:52,373
Many Pods share a node.

14
00:00:52,373 --> 00:00:59,577
Without requests, the scheduler cannot know whether a node is full and packs too much onto it; without limits,

15
00:00:59,577 --> 00:01:05,143
one leaking application can take all the memory and take its neighbours down with it.

16
00:01:05,143 --> 00:01:08,221
Requests and limits make a cluster predictable:

17
00:01:08,221 --> 00:01:12,936
each application gets what it asked for, and no more than it is allowed.

18
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:17,120
And how does it actually work?

19
00:01:17,667 --> 00:01:21,441
The scheduler adds up requests, not real usage:

20
00:01:21,441 --> 00:01:24,573
a node can be "full" while mostly idle.

21
00:01:24,573 --> 00:01:31,960
Memory cannot be taken back from a process, so the only answer to too much memory is a kill.

22
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:36,297
CPU can be shared, so too much CPU only means waiting.

23
00:01:36,297 --> 00:01:39,509
QoS class, set from requests and limits:

24
00:01:39,509 --> 00:01:43,524
Guaranteed (requests = limits for CPU and memory),

25
00:01:43,524 --> 00:01:50,751
Burstable (some requests), BestEffort (none, killed first when a node runs out of memory).

26
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:52,678
1.

27
00:01:52,678 --> 00:01:55,429
A Pod with requests and limits:

28
00:01:55,967 --> 00:01:56,122
2.

29
00:01:56,122 --> 00:01:58,600
Its QoS class and its resources:

30
00:01:58,600 --> 00:01:59,374
Burstable:

31
00:01:59,374 --> 00:02:02,704
requests are set but lower than the limits.

32
00:02:02,704 --> 00:02:02,859
3.

33
00:02:02,859 --> 00:02:06,266
What the node has reserved for all its Pods:

34
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:07,556
Burstable:

35
00:02:07,556 --> 00:02:10,807
requests are set but lower than the limits.

36
00:02:10,807 --> 00:02:10,958
3.

37
00:02:10,958 --> 00:02:14,284
What the node has reserved for all its Pods:

38
00:02:16,033 --> 00:02:18,943
How do I see what Kubernetes did?

39
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:27,709
Current usage needs the metrics-server add-on (lesson 26 enables it); requests and limits are always in the spec:

40
00:02:29,467 --> 00:02:31,299
Let me try something.

41
00:02:31,299 --> 00:02:36,361
A container whose memory keeps growing, with a 32Mi limit:

42
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:39,077
What should happen?

43
00:02:39,077 --> 00:02:41,254
The container runs.

44
00:02:41,254 --> 00:02:42,859
What happened?

45
00:02:42,859 --> 00:02:44,005
OOMKilled.

46
00:02:45,767 --> 00:02:47,423
For a real application:

47
00:02:47,423 --> 00:02:52,679
raise the limit to what it really needs (measure first), or fix the leak.

48
00:02:52,679 --> 00:02:59,736
Our tail grows forever, so no limit is enough; replace it with a bounded command and give it room:

49
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:02,213
Got it.

50
00:03:02,213 --> 00:03:04,965
What do I need to remember?

51
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:11,964
Requests decide where a Pod can run (scheduling); limits decide how much it may use.

52
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:20,635
Over the memory limit → OOMKilled, exit code 137; over the CPU limit → throttled.

53
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:26,950
Pending with Insufficient cpu/memory means requests do not fit on any node.

54
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:32,183
I keep hearing about scheduling basics.

55
00:03:32,183 --> 00:03:33,880
What is it, really?

56
00:03:34,433 --> 00:03:37,663
Scheduling is choosing a node for a new Pod.

57
00:03:37,663 --> 00:03:42,580
The scheduler (lesson 02) filters out the nodes that cannot run it,

58
00:03:42,580 --> 00:03:46,324
scores the rest and writes the winner into the Pod.

59
00:03:46,324 --> 00:03:48,525
You influence its choice with:

60
00:03:48,525 --> 00:03:50,654
node labels and nodeSelector:

61
00:03:50,654 --> 00:03:57,186
"only on nodes labelled disktype=ssd" (the Pod chooses the node); taints and tolerations:

62
00:03:57,186 --> 00:04:02,838
a node says "keep away unless you tolerate lab=demo" (the node rejects Pods).

63
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:05,039
Nodes are not all equal:

64
00:04:05,039 --> 00:04:10,911
some have GPUs or fast disks, some are reserved for one team or for system components,

65
00:04:10,911 --> 00:04:13,573
some are being drained for maintenance.

66
00:04:13,573 --> 00:04:20,469
Labels and selectors send Pods where they belong; taints keep the wrong Pods away from special nodes.

67
00:04:22,233 --> 00:04:24,653
And how does it actually work?

68
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:27,388
nodeSelector is a hard rule:

69
00:04:27,388 --> 00:04:29,888
no matching node, no scheduling.

70
00:04:29,888 --> 00:04:32,701
(nodeAffinity is the richer version:

71
00:04:32,701 --> 00:04:38,718
preferences, In/NotIn expressions.) A taint has a key, a value and an effect:

72
00:04:38,718 --> 00:04:43,719
NoSchedule (new Pods without a toleration are not placed there),

73
00:04:43,719 --> 00:04:49,501
PreferNoSchedule (avoid if possible), NoExecute (also evict running Pods).

74
00:04:49,501 --> 00:04:55,830
A toleration only allows a Pod onto a tainted node; it does not attract it there.

75
00:04:55,830 --> 00:05:06,379
Control-plane nodes in real clusters carry the taint node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule; Minikube's single node does not,

76
00:05:06,379 --> 00:05:08,254
so it can run your Pods.

77
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:10,173
1.

78
